opposition leaders in belarus have  welcomed a decision  by eu leaders to reject the results of  the election  that kept president alexander lukashenko  in power  eu heads also announced sanctions  against a large number of individuals  involved in alleged voting fraud and  violence against protesters  this is not lunchtime or the end of a  shift  this is another nail in the coffin of  lukashenko's credibility  workers walk out of a state-run  chemicals factory  threats have no power over them anymore  protesters urged workers at one of the  country's famed tractor factories to  join them  but some decided to keep their jobs  these miners were already outside at a  rally  across the industrial heartlands of  belarus  rejection of lukashenko's regime has  gone nationwide  and on wednesday that rejection  officially went international  a clear message from the european union  we are ready to accompany peaceful  democratic transition of power in  belarus  germany's angela merkel has spoken with  russia's putin  but said lukashenko wouldn't take her  calls  one can only mediate if one is in  contact with all sides  but we will do everything we can from  our side to promote a national dialogue  in lukashenko security council meeting  the talk was  of no talk the leaders of the western  countries offer us negotiations  conversations  and at this time they continue to push  their line we can see it we understand  it  but we do not accept it i want them to  hear that  but those filling the streets across  belarus day and night are desperate for  dialogue  one of their leaders told dw they want  everyone to help  if it's possible that the eu states the  russian federation and ukraine can help  us to organize a dialogue within the  country  and with external partners so that a  transfer of power happens  and we listen to each other i would  always support it  this is a decisive moment for us  decisive not only because putin has  remained tight-lipped  but decisive because they have the  support of the european union  and decisive because now their fear has  gone  and for more let's bring in  correspondent nick connolly who is  joining us  from minsk nick what is the atmosphere  among protesters do they  sense change in the air  well you definitely get a sense that  people have lost their fear fear of  coming out in the streets  um simply because there are so many of  them i think there's a feeling that the  regime here can't put all of them in  jail having said that there has been  significant violence protesters spending  days in  uh prison in detention about 30 people  still unaccounted for days after they  were taken in  but people are coming out are voicing  their dissatisfaction to regime in a way  that was unthinkable just a few weeks  ago  think about these protests they're very  diffused there is no central protest  camp these protests  um erupt all across minsk and all across  the country and disappear as quickly  as they begin i think this is part of  the strategy to make it more  difficult for the security services here  to come after people  and to detain them but there is a real  sense that this is  far from over this is the beginning  rather than the end of a process  of uh coming out and uh voicing their  dissatisfaction after 26 years with just  one president  and what kind of reaction has there been  to the planned european sanctions  well yesterday on the main square here  in minsk you  were aware of thousands of protesters  were out for yet another protest in  front of the government buildings  there was a sense that people had taken  this uh to notice they were happy to  realize that europe  is watching and paying attention to  these protests i think it's more a  symbolic step  it shows that europe is solidarity to an  extent with them without making any  particularly  strong promises i think in terms of  practical influence on the regime here  i don't think it will make that much of  a difference lukashenko has been  under sanctions time and time again and  those sanctions have then been withdrawn  without him doing much in return  i think alexander lukchenko's government  will affected those in but definitely on  the  symbol on the the kind of gesture  politics side of things that was  important step for people here on the  streets to know that they are  being watched and that the events here  are being followed nick bring us up to  date  on where russia stands in all of this  because  it has traditionally been a very close  ally of lukashenkos  exactly i mean belarus is russia's  closest ally they  are currently so-called union states  they have a kind of  loose uh association which russia has  always wanted to deepen to further  integrate belarus  into effectively one country with russia  um  president lexington has spoken to  vladimir putin several times this week  and been assured of russia's willingness  to  support him should events escalate here  um i think it's a difficult uh game for  lukashenko because he has tried to  resist  full integration with russia he fears  that russia will effectively take over  and he will be left maybe only as some  symbolic  figurehead the russians for now are not  saying that they will bring in troops  they are  supporting the lukashenko regime with  advisers bella russian state tv  yesterday  it was reported that many of the local  journalists have been replaced with  russian journalists  because the locals were no longer loyal  to the regime so i think it's about  deniable support for the lukashenko  regime  not russian tanks out in the streets of  mint but instead stuff that  you know events and support through  people and resources that can shore up  this regime without  bringing that high diplomatic cost for  the kremlin without coming out in kind  of full-scale  support with boots on the ground as it  were that is what we're going to see in  the next  days and weeks okay so it's all very  covert at this point dw's nick connolly  with the view from minsk  thank you nick
 

